---
source: crates/hibi-cli/tests/snapshots.rs
expression: "help(&repo, &[command, \"--help\"])"
---
$ hibi mark --help
exit status: 0
---- standard output ----
Add a mark to each claim: a record that you checked the statement again against the current content and it still holds. The claims become fresh.

Every side must resolve to one unit. If a side is missing or matches several units, use `hibi reanchor` to point it at the right unit. If the statement is no longer true, fix the text or use `hibi retire`.

The result gives, under "accepted", the state each claim was in before the mark and the sides that were not the same then.

With --stdin, standard input is a JSON array, written all or not at all. Each element is a claim id or {"claim": "<id>", "note": "...", "by": "..."}. `hibi schema mark-input` prints the schema.

Usage: hibi mark [OPTIONS] [claim-id]...

Arguments:
  [claim-id]...
          The claims that were checked again. An id may be shortened to a unique prefix

Options:
      --note <text>
          A note stored with each mark

      --by <text>
          Who checked, stored with each mark for the record [default: the value of HIBI_BY]

      --stdin
          Read a JSON array of claim ids or objects from standard input. No other mark argument is allowed with it

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --root <dir>
          Use this directory as the root (the directory that contains .hibi/) instead of searching upward from the working directory

      --format <format>
          Output format. Without this option the output is JSON when standard output is not a terminal, and text when it is

          Possible values:
          - human: Text for a person
          - json:  One JSON object

      --now <timestamp>
          Use this instant as the current time, for reproducible runs. RFC 3339 with any offset, for example 2026-11-20T18:04:11Z. A fraction of a second is dropped. The largest value is 9999-12-30T22:00:00Z

      --no-cache
          Do not read or write the cache database. Every command that does not call the judge gives the same result with and without the cache. The judge commands (triage, suggest-evidence, status --judge-order) may rank differently from one run to the next without it
